WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.240
Passive DAST checks observe application behavior without intentionally mutating requests in a way designed to provoke vulnerabilities.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:17.780
They may inspect response headers, cookies, transport settings, error pages, cache behavior, exposed server details, redirects, and configuration signals.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.280
Passive checks are usually lower risk, but they still require scope and authorization because they involve interacting with a target environment.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.100
Active checks send controlled test inputs or modified requests to evaluate runtime behavior.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:41.640
The purpose is to see whether the application validates input, handles unexpected values safely, protects sessions, avoids unsafe errors, and enforces security controls.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.780
In this course, active testing is discussed conceptually, without exploit strings or destructive procedures.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.680
The stable principle is controlled testing inside approved scope.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.220
Rate, volume, and state control are part of safe active testing.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:05.080
Scanners can generate many requests, create noisy logs, trigger rate limits, fill test data stores, or affect workflows if not configured carefully.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:16.100
Approved test windows, request throttling, excluded endpoints, non-destructive test accounts, and monitoring coordination help reduce operational risk while preserving useful coverage.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.320
Active testing should avoid destructive operations and protect test data.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:31.020
If a workflow sends emails, charges accounts, deletes records, creates tickets, or notifies customers, it needs special handling or exclusion unless explicitly approved.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.040
The goal is not to prove the scanner can touch everything.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.300
The goal is to produce useful runtime evidence without causing avoidable business impact.
